In the previous sections, we covered how to properly build ICE charts and the fact that each chemical equilibrium has an associated polynomial. The roots of these polynomials are related to the extent $x$ of the reaction at equilibrium. As such, it was critical to be able to obtain polynomial roots by means of a graphing calculator. Now, we will address the use of ICE charts to study reactions at equilibrium, and we will describe not only how to explicitly obtain the associated polynomial, but also how to use its roots to obtain chemical information.